//? Title: Progressive GAN: Two Fade Paths and Eight Optimizer States
//? Subtitle: A complete native 4x4-to-8x8 training schedule with checked phase ownership
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A tiny generator grows from4x4 features to8x8 images while its discriminator grows
in the reverse direction. The generator blends old and new sigmoid images; the
discriminator blends old and new4x4 feature maps before their shared nonlinear
core. Eight caller-owned parameter groups keep independent Adam states, and the
three stages choose exactly which groups receive writes. The complete C++ program
prepares MNIST through datamunge, retains all96 batches and reproduces the source
checkpoints, derivatives, galleries and losses. Three compact epochs leave weak,
nearly uniform generated patches. This is a reproducible explanation of growth
and update ownership, with no claim of a successful digit generator.
)
\toc

> Grow the two networks at different locations in their forward paths

[Karras, Aila, Laine and Lehtinen, Progressive Growing of GANs for Improved Quality,
Stability, and Variation (ICLR2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10196) introduces
progressive growth. This repository's small teaching model retains two resolutions,
pixel RMS normalization in G, and an explicit fade. It uses biased normal(.02)
convolution kernels, a tanh dense stem, sigmoid image outputs and least-squares
objectives. It does not reproduce the paper's full architecture or evaluation.
All2067 values in24 tensors are initialized before the first update. Growth
means activating a path and its optimizer groups; it does not allocate new
parameters partway through fitting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/progressive_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
