> Normalize channels within one pixel, without centering or batch state

For C channels at one pixel, let r=sqrt(mean(x_c^2)+epsilon) and y_c=x_c/r.
For x=(3,4), r is approximately sqrt(12.5), so y is approximately(.848528,1.131371).
The mean squared output is near1; the Euclidean length is near sqrt(2).
There is no subtraction of a channel mean, learned affine transform or moving
batch statistic. Different rows and spatial positions have separate RMS values.

For a supplied output adjoint v, the input adjoint is
(v-y*mean(v*y))/r. The native division/square-root graph keeps this derivative
and its higher derivatives live. The source epsilon is double(1e-8f), including
its Float32 rounding before conversion to Double. Zero pixels remain zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/progressive_pixel_rms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n     = dm::nn;
  auto   x        = n::tensor({3., 4.}, {1, 1, 1, 2}, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  auto   y        = n::pixel_rms_normalize(x);
  auto   grad     = n::grad(n::sum(y), {x})[0];
  double r        = std::sqrt(12.5 + double(1e-8f));
  auto   expected = (n::ones_like(y) - y * n::mean(y, {3}, true)) / r;
  if (n::max(n::abs(grad - expected)).item() > 1e-14)
    throw std::runtime_error("pixel RMS adjoint");
  std::print(
      "Output={}, mean(square)={}, sum-output adjoint={}\n",
      y.values_str(),
      n::mean(n::square(y)).item(),
      grad.values_str());
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(0, 6)
      .lines({1, 2}, {3, 4}, "Input channels", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points({1, 2}, {3, 4}, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .lines({1, 2}, y.to_vector<double>(), "Pixel RMS", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points({1, 2}, y.to_vector<double>(), "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .x_label("Channel index at one supplied pixel")
      .y_label("Channel value")
      .title("Pixel RMS normalization changes scale, without centering")
      .save_svg("build/progressive_pixel_rms.svg");
}
```
