\alttext(Two channel values3 and4 divide by their shared RMS and become about.85 and1.13.)

> Fade images after the two nonlinear RGB outputs

Write O=nearest(sigmoid(old_RGB(core))) and N=sigmoid(new_RGB(new_block(core))).
Then G_alpha=(1-alpha)*O+alpha*N. Holding all weights and z fixed gives the
pointwise derivative dG/dalpha=N-O. With supplied endpoint pixels.2 and.8, the
midpoint is.5 and its alpha derivative is.6. This blend occurs after sigmoid;
blending logits before sigmoid would define a different path.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/progressive_image_fade.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<double> alpha, values;
  for (int k = 0; k <= 20; ++k) {
    auto a     = n::full({}, k / 20., n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
    auto old   = n::full({1, 4, 4, 1}, .2, n::DType::Float64),
         fresh = n::full({1, 8, 8, 1}, .8, n::DType::Float64);
    auto image = (1. - a) * n::upsample_nearest(old, 2) + a * fresh;
    auto da    = n::grad(n::mean(image), {a})[0];
    if (std::abs(da.item() - .6) > 1e-14)
      throw std::runtime_error("post-sigmoid image fade");
    alpha.push_back(k / 20.);
    values.push_back(n::mean(image).item());
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(0, 1.2)
      .lines(alpha, values, "Image blend", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .x_label("Alpha, with endpoint images held fixed")
      .y_label("Blended pixel value")
      .title("A linear image fade has derivative new minus old")
      .save_svg("build/progressive_image_fade.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/progressive_image_fade.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Only the supplied endpoint tensors are used here. The later gallery changes alpha with the final fitted weights and fixed noise.)
\alttext(A supplied pixel increases linearly from.2 at alpha0 to.8 at alpha1.)

> Fade discriminator features before their shared nonlinear core

Let L=leaky(old_RGB(pool2(image))) and H=pool2(new_block(leaky(new_RGB(image)))).
D_alpha(image)=core((1-alpha)*L+alpha*H), where core contains a3x3 conv,
LeakyReLU(.2), flattening and a raw linear score. D has no pixel normalization.
It is generally false that D_alpha equals the same blend of endpoint scores.

A scalar hand check supplies L=-1,H=1 and a core consisting of LeakyReLU(.2).
The actual path is leaky(2*alpha-1); blending the endpoint scores instead gives
1.2*alpha-.2. At alpha.5 these are0 and.4. Both paths have the same endpoints,
so endpoint checks alone cannot catch the wrong insertion point.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/progressive_feature_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<double> alpha, feature, score;
  for (int k = 0; k <= 20; ++k) {
    double a     = k / 20.;
    auto   mixed = n::full({1, 1}, 2 * a - 1, n::DType::Float64);
    alpha.push_back(a);
    feature.push_back(n::leaky_relu(mixed, .2).item());
    score.push_back(1.2 * a - .2);
  }
  if (feature[10] != 0 || std::abs(score[10] - .4) > 1e-14)
    throw std::runtime_error("nonlinear fade location");
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(-.3, 1.6)
      .lines(alpha, feature, "Feature then core", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(alpha, score, "Endpoint scores", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .x_label("Alpha, with old/new features held fixed")
      .y_label("Supplied one-feature discriminator score")
      .title("The shared nonlinear core follows the feature fade")
      .save_svg("build/progressive_feature_toy.svg");
}
```
