> A zero current gradient can still produce an Adam write

With beta1=.5 and beta2=.999, stored first and second moments persist. The toy
below starts one supplied scalar at1 and explicitly steps gradients1,0,0 at
learning rate.0002. The last two steps still change it. The actual model has
one Adam state per group, epsilon1e-8, and independent bias-correction counters.
Skipping an inactive group means making no call to step, rather than passing it
a vector of zeros. This distinction preserves the original stage semantics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/progressive_adam.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore p(1, n::DType::Float64);
  p.get("supplied", {1}, n::init::ones());
  n::OptimizerConfig c;
  c.kind    = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
  c.beta1   = .5;
  c.beta2   = .999;
  c.epsilon = 1e-8;
  n::Optimizer        adam(p, .0002, c);
  std::vector<double> step{0}, value{p.at("supplied").item()};
  for (double g : {1., 0., 0.}) {
    adam.step({n::full({1}, g, n::DType::Float64)});
    step.push_back(adam.iterations());
    value.push_back(p.at("supplied").item());
  }
  if (!(value[3] < value[2] && value[2] < value[1]))
    throw std::runtime_error("Adam moment continuation");
  std::print(
      "Supplied gradients1,0,0 give values: {}, {}, {}, {}\n",
      value[0],
      value[1],
      value[2],
      value[3]);
  std::vector<double> decrease;
  for (double v : value)
    decrease.push_back(10000 * (1 - v));
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(-.4, 6)
      .lines(step, decrease, "Grads1,0,0", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(step, decrease, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .x_label("Optimizer iteration, independent of any stage")
      .y_label("Decrease from initial1, multiplied by10000")
      .title("Zero current gradient still uses stored Adam moments")
      .save_svg("build/progressive_adam.svg");
}
```
